                <head>216. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On August 30, Prime Minister Eden notified President Eisenhower that Great
                    Britain would be sending to Canton Island two long-range aircraft which would
                    “carry out a photographic reconnaissance of certain British islands in the
                    Central Pacific which may be suitable as sites for testing thermo-nuclear
                    weapons,” and asked the President to inform Lewis L. Strauss, Chairman of the
                    Atomic Energy Commission, and Admiral Arthur W. Radford, Chairman of the Joint
                    Chiefs of Staff, of the purpose of these flights. In his reply on August 31, the
                    President assured the Prime Minister “that this government has no objection to
                    your carrying on the aerial explorations from Canton Island that you have
                    described.” (Eisenhower Library, Whitman File, International File)</p>
            </div>
